<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Takamatsu Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1588 CE – 1871 CE · capital Takamatsu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A domain of Sanuki on Shikoku, ruled by a branch of the Tokugawa family and known for Ritsurin garden and its fine sugar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanuki-province">Sanuki</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in 1587 and in 1588 began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> castle, a sea castle with moats fed by seawater. The Ikoma kept the province after 1600, but a family dispute led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> to remove them in 1640.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1642 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yorishige">Matsudaira Yorishige</a>, a son of the Mito Tokugawa founder, was given eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanuki-province">Sanuki</a> with 120,000 koku, and his line ruled until the end of the period as close kin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>. The domain developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsurin-garden">Ritsurin garden</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yoritaka">Matsudaira Yoritaka</a> encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane in the mid-18th century, leading to fine wasanbon sugar, and large salt pans followed in the 19th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi">Toba-Fushimi</a> in 1868 and was occupied and punished by the new government. After its abolition in 1871 the area became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/takamatsu-domain.jpg" alt="Takamatsu Domain" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takamatsu_castle09s3872.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1588 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Takamatsu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ikoma Chikamasa, Matsudaira Yorishige, Matsudaira Yoritaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Takamatsu han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=takamatsu-domain" aria-label="Takamatsu Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8089 934 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Takamatsu Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8409 1190L8530 1145M8409 1190L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8409 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8409 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Takamatsu. <a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=takamatsu-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
